⟦P0⟧Tutorial: Accessing a relational database⟦P1⟧This tutorial introduces the basics of accessing a relational database with⟦P2⟧Go and the ⟦P3⟧ package in its standard library.⟦P4⟧You'll get the most out of this tutorial if you have a basic familiarity with⟦P5⟧Go and its tooling. If this is your first exposure to Go, please see⟦P6⟧⟦P7⟧Tutorial: Get started with Go⟦P8⟧⟦P9⟧for a quick introduction.⟦P10⟧The ⟦P11⟧⟦P12⟧⟦P13⟧ package you'll⟦P14⟧be using includes types and functions for connecting to databases, executing⟦P15⟧transactions, canceling an operation in progress, and more. For more details⟦P16⟧on using the package, see⟦P17⟧⟦P18⟧Accessing databases⟦P19⟧.⟦P20⟧In this tutorial, you'll create a database, then write code to access the⟦P21⟧database. Your example project will be a repository of data about vintage⟦P22⟧jazz records.⟦P23⟧In this tutorial, you'll progress through the following sections:⟦P24⟧Create a folder for your code.⟦P25⟧Set up a database.⟦P26⟧Import the database driver.⟦P27⟧Get a database handle and connect.⟦P28⟧Query for multiple rows.⟦P29⟧Query for a single row.⟦P30⟧Add data.⟦P31⟧⟦P32⟧Note:⟦P33⟧ For other tutorials, see ⟦P34⟧Tutorials⟦P35⟧.⟦P36⟧Prerequisites ⟦P37⟧⟦P38⟧⟦P39⟧An installation of the ⟦P40⟧⟦P41⟧⟦P42⟧⟦P43⟧relational database management system (⟦P44⟧).⟦P45⟧⟦P46⟧⟦P47⟧An installation of Go.⟦P48⟧ For installation instructions, see⟦P49⟧⟦P50⟧Installing Go⟦P51⟧.⟦P52⟧⟦P53⟧A tool to edit your code.⟦P54⟧ Any text editor you have will work fine.⟦P55⟧⟦P56⟧A command terminal.⟦P57⟧ Go works well using any terminal on Linux and Mac,⟦P58⟧and on ⟦P59⟧ or cmd in Windows.⟦P60⟧Create a folder for your code ⟦P61⟧⟦P62⟧To begin, create a folder for the code you'll write.⟦P63⟧Open a command prompt and change to your home directory.⟦P64⟧On Linux or Mac:⟦P65⟧On Windows:⟦P66⟧For the rest of the tutorial we will show a $ as the prompt. The⟦P67⟧commands we use will work on Windows too.⟦P68⟧From the command prompt, create a directory for your code called⟦P69⟧data-acces